   "id": "adjustment_for_uncertainty_around_cyp_estimates_best_guess",
   "label": "Adjustment for uncertainty around CYP estimates (Best guess)",
   "section": "cea",
   "value": -0.1,
   "kind": "estimate",
   "source": {
    "title": "Source spreadsheet",
    "url": "https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/13zNeNX3hm1ZQDvvg5v_NACifYw7ryPVIOhCPez4XQAs/edit",
    "cell": "CEA!B20"
   },
   "explainer": "Guess. Couple-years of protection (CYP) conversion factors are implictly (and explicitly in some cases) intended to capture effects such as methods not being used correctly, early stopping of some methods (long-acting reversible contraceptives), and overlapping method use. We explicitly apply a -10% adjustment for higher than average failure rates of contraception methods in the units of value per CYP we estimate: \"We assume that women who take up modern contraception due to GiveWell-funded programs have 10% higher failure rates when using modern contraceptives relative to the typical user of modern contraception. This adjustment is meant to capture that the women who take up due to our programs are likely to be more inexperienced using modern contraception than the average user.\" \nHowever, we have not fully vetted the extent to which CYP conversion factors are accounting for overlapping",
   "format": "percent"
  },
